% ============================================================================
% speleothem-growth-surface — which cave surface a named dripstone
% speleothem grows from (adj-facts-stdlib, EARTH-SCIENCE).
% ============================================================================
% "Which one hangs from the ceiling — the stalactite or the stalagmite?" is
% the single most-confused pair in elementary Earth science, and it is
% exactly the kind of question that should be answered from a citation
% rather than from a mnemonic. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? speleothem_growth_surface(stalactite, $S)
%       % cave_ceiling
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `speleothem_growth_surface(speleothem, surface)` carrying the
% source citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query —
% the engine returns the surface WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on any speleothem the source does not place on
% a single growth surface. The query also runs BACKWARD, which is the
% direction the confusion actually runs in:
%
%     ? speleothem_growth_surface($P, cave_floor)
%       % stalagmite
%
% FIRST cave/karst library in this stdlib — a full-tree grep for
% `speleothem`, `stalactite`, `stalagmite`, `dripstone` and `flowstone`
% across every shipped `.adj` returned nothing beforehand, so there is
% nothing here to duplicate. It joins the existing earth-science/ and
% geology/ families (rock types, weathering causes, soil horizons, the
% sedimentary formation sequence) on an axis none of them covers.
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from the U.S. National Park Service's
% "Speleothems" article in its Caves and Karst subject series:
%
%     speleothem (this table's atom)   surface (this table's atom)   quote (verbatim)
%     ------------------------------   --------------------------    ---------------------------------------------------
%     stalactite                       cave_ceiling                  "Stalactites are the most common and most familiar
%                                                                       of all speleothems; they resemble icicles or
%                                                                       carrots hanging from cave ceilings."
%     stalagmite                       cave_floor                    "Stalagmites are convex floor deposits built up by
%                                                                       water dripping from an overhead stalactite or
%                                                                       from the cave ceiling."
%
% curl-fetched the raw page and read it byte-for-byte before writing this
% file (not just an AI-summarized WebFetch); both sentences were confirmed
% present character-for-character, and the page was CONTENT-verified — not
% merely status-checked — as a substantive article (118 sentences, no
% soft-404 markers, no hub/navigation shape) rather than a landing page.
% `trust authoritative` — the National Park Service is a first-party .gov
% publisher of exactly the fact being recalled, and the same source
% `earth-science/weathering-cause-type.adj` already cites for a different
% Earth process.
%
% Honest abstention — and the abstentions carry as much of this file's
% content as the two rows do:
%
%   - `column` is NOT a row, and the source says why in its own words:
%     "Columns are not stalactites nor are they stalagmites; they are
%     both, together." A column forms when "a stalagmite grows together
%     with its counterpart feeder stalactite" — it is produced by two
%     speleothems JOINING, not by growing from one surface, so it has no
%     single growth surface to bind. Recording it as `cave_ceiling` or
%     `cave_floor` would be false, and recording both would misrepresent
%     the relation this table holds.
%   - `helictite` is NOT a row either, though the source does place it:
%     "Helictites grow on cave ceilings, walls, and less often on cave
%     floors." Three surfaces, with the source's own frequency hedge on
%     the third. Tabling one surface would silently drop the others;
%     tabling all three as equals would flatten the "less often" the
%     source deliberately states. It needs a different shape than this
%     table's one-surface-per-speleothem relation, so it abstains here
%     rather than being distorted into it.
%   - `flowstone` and `draperies` are described by their APPEARANCE and
%     mode of deposition ("melted cake icing", "frozen waterfalls",
%     build-up "in layers or bands", a line down a sloped ceiling) rather
%     than by a growth surface, so neither has a value for this relation.
%   - `soda_straw` is the hollow tube every stalactite BEGINS as ("All
%     stalactites, whatever their composition, begin their growth as
%     hollow soda straws") — a developmental stage of a stalactite rather
%     than a separately-placed speleothem, and a candidate for its own
%     future table on a different axis.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table speleothem_growth_surface {
    columns speleothem, surface

    row (stalactite, cave_ceiling)
    row (stalagmite, cave_floor)

    source "Stalactites are the most common and most familiar of all speleothems; they resemble icicles or carrots hanging from cave ceilings."
    locator "https://www.nps.gov/subjects/caves/speleothems.htm"
    trust authoritative

    cites "Stalagmites are convex floor deposits built up by water dripping from an overhead stalactite or from the cave ceiling." locator "https://www.nps.gov/subjects/caves/speleothems.htm"
}
